    "result_text": "The full suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation for the create-owner pipeline.\n\n**The rule** lives where telephone knowledge already lives \u2014 `OwnerTelephones`:\n- Added a `NATIONAL_DIGITS` map (`\"1\" \u2192 10`, `\"61\" \u2192 9`) \u2014 country calling code to required national-digit count.\n- Added `hasValidNationalLength(String telephone)`: matches the E.164 number's country code (longest-first so `+61` isn't mistaken for `+6`/`+1`), then checks the remaining national digits equal the required count. An unknown country code carries no rule and is accepted, so the existing `+64` behaviour is untouched.\n\n**Enforcement** is a new single-responsibility step, `RequireTelephoneNationalLength`, wired into `owners.POST.yml` right after `normalizeTelephone` (so it sees the already-canonicalized E.164 value). On a wrong length it throws the new checked `InvalidTelephoneLengthException`, handled by `InvalidTelephoneLengthExceptionHandler` \u2192 **400**, following the exact pattern of the existing `InvalidEmail`/`InvalidTelephone` trios.\n\n`toE164` (format canonicalization) and `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` are left with their original single responsibility \u2014 I didn't fold the length rule into them.\n\nFiles:\n- `OwnerTelephones.java` \u2014 added the length data + `hasValidNationalLength`\n- `RequireTelephoneNationalLength.java` \u2014 new pipeline step\n- `InvalidTelephoneLengthException.java` / `...Handler.java` \u2014 new 400 exception + handler\n- `...InvalidTelephoneLengthException.yml` \u2014 escalation mapping\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 new `requireTelephoneLength` step in the pipeline\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest` (the new `+61 123` reject case) and the full suite pass \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The valid `04XXXXXXXX` payload (\u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) and the `+1` seed data still satisfy the rule.",
